 or stricter norms  and it has a lot to do with how much  threat that they experience so when we  published a paper in science a few years  ago we showed that nations that have a  lot of threat whether it's natural  disasters or it's invasions that they've  been threatened by over a hundred years  or  even disease prevalence they tend to  have much stronger norms and it makes a  lot of sense because when you have  threat you need tight rules and strong  leaders to help you coordinate to  survive we've been showing this in  computer models that when you have  threat tightness evolves and that's a  really important evolutionary principle  and instinct that we have as human  groups so to give us a sense like where  where does the u.s. rank on this in  terms of the scale so that we kind of  understand maybe that spectrum is well  it's interesting I mean the US has in  our data been typically more loose and  years on the loose side with some  pockets of tightness in different areas  in the US you know as a nation were  separated by two oceans from other  countries we haven't been worried about  being invaded for centuries by Mexico  and Canada and so we have evolved to  have looser norms there are states that  have tighter norms typically are in the  south and the Midwest as compared to the  coast and actually in our data you could  see that natural disasters and other  threats actually predict that level of  tightness so there's a there's also some  within culture variation there's also if  you zoom into States this variation in  social class and we often think about  social classes just being about bank  accounts but actually in our research  the working class is far tighter than  the upper class they want stronger rules  they need stronger rules because they  have much more threat whether it's  falling into poverty or it's working in  dangerous occupations or being in  neighborhoods where rules really help  keep kids out of trouble okay so now  we're getting into one 