 cross-cultural  psychologists because leaders can  actually amplify an exaggerated threat  target people who are more threatened  and use that to gain popularity and it's  a very powerful tactic I've actually  brought people into my own lab and I've  actually manipulated fake threats like  terrorism or natural disasters or  pathogen prevalence and you could see  within moments people tighten up in the  lab they want stronger rules and I start  to become more ethnocentric and that's  exactly what leaders around the world  are doing you see it not just in the  u.s. not just in France you see it in  Italy you seen a hungry there using a  tight blues playbook actually using  terms like we have Muslim invaders or  fun with say in Hungary where our  culture is being destroyed they're  saying in Italy and these are very  strong words and they tap into this  instinct of tightness where people who  believe that will instantly support  stronger rules and stronger leaders so  you mentioned the the kind of  evolutionary angle I mean is it is we  could all imagine I guess how on on the  savanna if you believe that you are  threatened by lions for example you are  definitely going to be motivated by that  fear to a point or allow the leader to  have a particular personality and to  sort of manage the people around them in  a particular way is is how strong is  that evolutionary component to this and  then what else has culture sort of added  to it yeah so we have that a lot of  evidence for that very principle we can  measure threat actual how much threat  groups face so in the study I did across  30 plus nations we measured how many  times as a nation been potentially  invaded by its neighbors objectively how  many natural disasters has a has a  country actually had to face in terms of  mother nature's fury  or famines by the way we did this also  with traditional societies over 80  societies that exist in the ancient  archaeological record where we can  actually assess how much threat did they  have in terms of famine and in terms of  invasions and how strong are norms do  they have how tight were their norms how  much do they have autocratic leaders and  the connection is the same whether it's  modern nations or traditional societies  or the u.s. 50 states it's a very strong  connection when you have threat you need  stronger rules to survive and the  leaders that can help provide those  rules and enforce them it's just that  that principle can get hijacked by  leaders and it's happened you know not  just nowadays and I gotta mentioned  centuries it's been happening where  leaders can use that psychology in order  to win elections there are some places  I've been to like for example  Switzerland strikes me as an example  that seems to be kind of an unusual mix  in terms of this spectrum of kind of  individualism versus collectivism but  also in terms of kind of social norms I  mean I guess question one is are there  am I accurately identifying that  something's a little bit different in  Switzerland maybe on the spectrum that  you've identified and and how do we  understand places like that Austria  maybe is another example yeah I think  it's a really good question and I'll  just back up and say all cultures have  both  loose elements just we have more or less  of them and so you can zoom into the  United States and find even though it's  generally has been loose you can have C  domains that we pretty are still pretty  strict about for example privacy is  something we pretty tightly regulate in  the United States it's a really core  value or other countries that will  tightly subscribe norms around other  important values what's interesting is  in our research we really see a big  difference between tightness and things  like collectivism so collectivism is  about your connection with your family  with your in groups tightness is about  the strength of norms in the cultural  context and punishments for deviating  from norms and you can 