e you know is this real is  this threat because really another  problem that I see with this dynamic is  that tight loose has a lot of trade-offs  for groups tight groups have a lot of  order they have a lot of uniformity and  they have a lot of self control people  behaving themselves that's really  important when you have a lot of threat  but loose groups who have less threat  tends to be more creative and the more  adaptable more flexible and so that when  you get to extreme levels of tightness  especially arbitrary levels of tightness  you're losing out on creativity and  adaptability so we have to really  negotiate the level of objective threat  that we're experiencing or else we're  gonna lose off on a lot of advantages  we've had as a society which is a  tremendous amount of creativity and  flexibility when you have tightness you  tend to have order but also  ethnocentrism people who look different  who act different who seem different  seem threatening and people are very  discriminatory against them both in  nations in traditional societies and in  states we show we have evidence for this  very directly so I think that's why we  have to really try to negotiate this  fear you know the big book of the of the  fall is fear in the White House what we  really have to be talking about is fear  outside of the White House people have  to be constantly challenging the  perception that everything is a disaster  we've been speaking with Michele Gelfand  who's a distinguished University  professor at the University of Maryland  leading expert on cultural differences  her new book is called rule makers rule  breakers how tight and loose cultures  wire our world Michele thank you so much  for talking to me about this today  thanks a lot  and now please see my website and send  me some type moose stories if you have  some

